[b][size=5]StarRupture ModLoader[/size][/b]

[b]Found a bug? Tell us on the Discord: [url=https://discord.gg/G5a4GYsvrz]https://discord.gg/G5a4GYsvrz[/url][/b] and attach your [b]ModLoader.log[/b] (in [b]Binaries\Win64\ModLoader\Logs\[/b]).

[b][size=4]Get your Free StarRupture Server with this Mod Loader already installed over at [url=http://www.alienxservers.co.uk]https://www.alienxservers.co.uk[/url][/size][/b]

[b]Open the Mod Loader window:[/b] press [b]Esc[/b] and pick [b]MOD LOADER[/b] below OPTIONS (the main menu has the row too), or press [b]F2[/b] (rebindable in modloader.ini). [b]Esc[/b] closes it. ✦

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/{{REPO}}/{{TAG}}/images/pause-menu.png[/img]

[line]

[b]The ModLoader Is Being Detected Via Machine Learning As A Trojan:[/b]
From [url=https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/wdsi/threats/malware-encyclopedia-description?name=Trojan%3AScript%2FWacatac.B!ml&threatid=2147735503]Microsoft[/url]:
Trojan:Script/Wacatac.B!ml is classified as a broad heuristic label. It captures a range of malicious activities from modular malware families that employ advanced scripting and evasion techniques. This detection is not a single virus, but a behavioral signature often triggered by unauthorized registry changes, in-memory code execution, and the misuse of trusted system processes.

It is a false positive, you can view the source code yourself or build the mod loader yourself if you do not trust the zip file here.

The mod loader relies on many of the same techniques used by game hacks, debuggers, overlays, and other software that interacts with running processes. These behaviors are necessary for the mod loader to function, but they can also trigger heuristic or behavioral detections from antivirus software.

[line]

[b][size=4]Support & Questions[/size][/b]
Join the official Mod Loader discord, [url=https://discord.gg/G5a4GYsvrz]AlienX's Place[/url] for support.

[line]

StarRupture ModLoader is a lightweight plugin-based mod loader for [i]StarRupture[/i].
It loads automatically using DLL proxy injection, meaning [b]no original game files are modified[/b].

The loader enables runtime fixes, server utilities, and gameplay improvements through optional plugins. On a client it adds an in-game window to manage them; on a dedicated server it adds a console.
A more detailed readme can be found on the [url=https://github.com/AlienXAXS/StarRupture-ModLoader/blob/main/README.md]github page[/url].

[line]

[b][size=6]What's new[/size][/b]

[list]
[*]A server can now turn a plugin feature off for the players on it. It keeps a list of plugin and feature pairs; a feature on the list is off whatever the game build allows, and comes back when it leaves the list. The new console command [b]features[/b] shows every feature with its state and manages the list. See Server rules below
[*]A server sends that list to its players: when a player's mod loader has sent its feature list, the server sends its list back, and the player's loader switches those features off within a moment, also for a plugin that loads or reloads later. A text the server sets with [b]features hostmessage[/b] goes with it into the player's ModLoader.log. A list a player set on their own PC is kept apart from the server's
[*]A server's ModLoader.log now warns once when a player joins on a different game build than the server's, for example "Player 'X' runs game build CL-130045, this server runs CL-127004". Nothing is refused
[*]The dedicated server now has the same version object map coverage as the game client: built-in rows for its game build (CL-127004) and its own signed version map file, so a server game update can be covered without a loader update. Server plugins that take their game data from the version map survive server updates
[*]The dedicated server now runs the same start check on the game's classes as the client, against a table made from the server's own game files. The client's check now has a table for each game build it supports (CL-127004 and CL-130045) and covers more of the game's classes (259 for CL-130045, 255 for CL-127004, where it was 159)
[*]The client now takes its game classes from the CL-130045 game files and still runs on CL-127004. The version object map keeps the rows for both builds
[*]If the game does not finish starting within 2 minutes on a game build the mod loader was not made for, the message now says so and what to check, instead of saying the game layout changed. The wait is now a true 2 minute limit; before, it could run much longer
[*]For plugin authors: a plugin can export [b]GetPluginWireInfo[/b] to list other versions of itself whose data it can read
[*]On the dedicated server, the HTTP method of a request now reaches plugins correctly, so the web panels of server plugins can POST again
[*]A message with a percent sign in it, such as a web address a plugin or the server logged, no longer risks a fault or a stuck log in the mod loader's log
[*]When a fault happens inside the game's world-end call, the mod loader still swallows it as before, and now writes the exception code, the fault address, the registers and a short call stack to ModLoader.log
[*]For plugin authors: plugin interface v79 adds IPluginHostRules (reached as hooks->HostRules) with SetRules, SetRulesForConnection, ClearConnection, RegisterOnClientFeatures, SetVersionMinimums, RegisterOnClientBelowMinimum, GetClientFeatures, AskAdmin, AdminMessage and RegisterOnAdminAnswer, for the server's loader only (on any other process the calls warn once and do nothing). These calls do nothing for a server host until a server plugin uses them. Plugins can also export the optional GetPluginWireInfo to say which other versions of themselves exchange data with them. The mod loader now accepts plugin interface 70 to 79
[*]New image library: mods that use it get item and building pictures from one place in the loader. See Image library below
[*]The mouse wheel no longer stops working after it was first used while a blocked mod key was held
[*]Keys a mod blocks are released when the mod is unloaded or reloaded, instead of staying blocked
[*]Mod overlay buttons can now be clicked while the build menu is open
[*]The build menu is now found on the game's menu layer, so a mod that waits for it to close sees it in every case
[*]A mod feature made for the newer game update now shows as "not in this game version" in the hook failures list on the current game version, instead of as a problem to report
[*]Better Drone's building drone, restore-state and hotbar functions are now rows of the version object map for both game builds (the hotbar is for the newer game update and does nothing on the current game version)
[*]The save and persistent ID functions the PersistentIdFix mod looks up are now rows of the version object map for both game builds, so the mod finds all of them on either one
[*]Mod windows no longer flicker while a save loads, and blink less after Alt-Tab
[*]After a graphics crash (the game losing its graphics device), the mod windows come back by themselves: they stay off for one launch as a precaution, and ModLoader.log says so. Before, they stayed off until you edited modloader.ini. If your mod windows are still off from an earlier version, set [b]Enabled=1[/b] under [b][UI][/b] in [i]ModLoader\modloader.ini[/i]
[*]ModLoader.log now records why the graphics device was lost and which step of a game icon copy failed, to help find the cause of a problem
[*]For plugin authors: plugins can read game pictures through the image library (plugin interface v77, ModLoader 1.24.0 or newer)
[*]Plugin textures and icons load much faster: a 256x256 upload went from about 29 ms to about 0.7 ms, and copying a game icon from about 29 ms to about 0.3 ms. The short freeze when the first texture loads is gone too
[*]Mod windows blink far less when the game gains or loses focus, for example after Alt-Tab
[*]When the game has updated ahead of the mod loader, the game keeps loading while the mod loader's message is up, instead of freezing for up to 3 minutes. The message stays on top of the game window
[*]A fresh install opens a window titled [b]Mod Loader Installed[/b] instead of [b]Plugins Updated[/b]
[*]Quieter log when adapting to new game versions: differences against another build's row in the version object map (v map) are Debug lines, not warnings. The v map maps each game version's objects (functions, fields, sizes) for easy access
[*]The loader skips version map rows it does not recognise instead of refusing the whole version map, so later versions of it can add new kinds of rows
[*]At every start the loader checks whether a newer version object map is out and, if so, uses it on that start, so a game update can be covered at once. The check is one small request that answers "nothing newer" in a few bytes, and if it fails (offline, a timeout, a bad file) the copy kept last time is used and the game starts as usual. The old once-a-day wait is gone
[*]For plugin authors: plugins can ask for the size of a game struct the game does not describe, a native member needs no size with PLUGIN_FIELD_ANY_SIZE, and plugins can ask where the game puts a function's parameters (also for Blueprint classes that load later, with the new PLUGIN_FIELD_LATE flag). The plugin interface version is unchanged
[*]For plugin authors: plugin interface v78 adds a call that draws many textured triangles at once, so a plugin with a large set of icons, a GPU-drawn map for example, needs one draw call instead of one per icon (ModLoader 1.25.0 or newer). Added by Kian369
[*]A mod that has to wait for the game's graphics while it shuts down can now be unloaded or reloaded: the loader no longer holds its own windows up while it waits, and the mod's row in the plugin list says it is being unloaded
[*]When the game updates to a build the version object map does not list yet, the mod loader no longer switches itself off. It runs with the newest known build's values, checks each one against your game before using it, and switches off only the mod features that do not check out. A window at the main menu says so. If a session on such a build crashes or is force-closed, the next start runs without mods and says how to try again. See Game updates below
[*]A mod made for the game build you are running is no longer shown as incompatible when a game update changed only some of the game classes the mod loader's start check reads. If a class every mod depends on changed, all mods still stay off. A mod that names no build, or another one, and does not take its game data from the version object map is still refused then
[/list]

[line]

[b][size=6]The Mod Loader Window (client)[/size][/b]

Open it from the MOD LOADER menu row or with F2, as above. The [b]~[/b] key (rebindable) opens the developer console. [b]Esc[/b] closes it, and [b]1[/b] to [b]5[/b] open Plugins, Config, Settings, Logging and Theme while the window has the focus (not while a text box, a slider drag or a keybind capture is taking keys). [b]Tab[/b] and the arrow keys move between buttons and fields, in plugin windows too (not over the game's own menus, where the keyboard stays with the game), and [b]Space[/b] or [b]Enter[/b] uses the one picked; the Settings tab can turn that off. ✦

Tabs down the left: [b]Plugins[/b], [b]Config[/b], [b]Settings[/b], [b]Logging[/b], [b]Theme[/b] and [b]About[/b] (the build tag and the plugins currently loaded). A splash window shows startup progress. In the main menu a bottom-left banner shows the build tag, plugin count and open key, plus a red note when an update is available.

[b][size=4]Plugins[/size][/b]

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/{{REPO}}/{{TAG}}/images/plugins-tab.png[/img]

[list]
[*][b]LOAD NEW[/b], beside the window title, picks up plugin DLLs added to the Plugins folder since the game started, and a popup lists each one with what to do next. [b]Loaded late[/b] registered for a world start that had already fired: load a save to catch it up. [b]Needs restart[/b] is a new DLL in the Preload folder. [b]Retry[/b] is a DLL still being copied or locked. The [b]rescan[/b] console command prints the same lines. ✦

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/{{REPO}}/{{TAG}}/images/popup-load-new.png[/img]

[*]Click a loaded plugin's row to open its page on the Config tab. The buttons on the row work as before. ✦
[*][b]Loaded (2 off)[/b] is a plugin running with two features turned off because a game update moved something they need. Hover the name or the count to see which; the plugin's settings for them grey out with the reason, and a [b]Plugin Features Off[/b] window lists them. ✦
[*]A plugin can say when it may be loaded and unloaded: any time, only at the main menu, or only at game start. A refused reload or unload says why on its row and on the console. ✦
[*][b]Crashed[/b] is a plugin that faulted in its own code. The mod loader switched it off and the game kept running; hover the row for the fault and where it happened. [b]RELOAD[/b] loads it fresh. A fault in game code a plugin called still crashes the game, and the log names the plugin. ✦
[*]When a plugin crashes, a toast in the bottom right corner names the plugin and what it was doing. About 3 seconds later the mod loader restarts it by itself, as RELOAD would, if its load and unload settings allow it; a plugin that needs a game restart stays off and the toast says so. Each plugin restarts at most 3 times a session. [b]Settings[/b] has [b]Restart crashed plugins automatically[/b] to turn it off. ✦
[*][b]Plugins Updated[/b] lists plugins with identical update notes on one line and shows the notes once, is wide enough for the screen at 4K, and keeps its title and Close button in view. After the mod loader updates, it lists the loader's release notes once, grouped by version and including the notes of releases you skipped. After a fresh install the same window is titled [b]Mod Loader Installed[/b]. ✦
[*][b]Cannot Load[/b] is a server plugin on a client, or the reverse.
[*][b]Needs Update[/b] names the side that is behind: the plugin or the mod loader.
[*][b]Incompatible[/b] on a game build other than the one a plugin was built for says the plugin needs an update. It still loads on its own build.
[*][b]WHY?[/b] on a Hooks Failed row reopens the report.
[*]Unload removes the panels, widgets and callbacks the plugin registered. If the overlay is still drawing the plugin's panel after about a second, the unload is skipped: the plugin is shut down and shows [b]Unloaded[/b], but its DLL stays mapped. [b]LOAD[/b] or [b]RELOAD[/b] retries.
[/list]

[b][size=4]Config[/size][/b]

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/{{REPO}}/{{TAG}}/images/config-tab.png[/img]

[list]
[*][b]Ctrl+Click[/b] a slider to type an exact value.
[*][b]-[/b] and [b]+[/b] beside a slider step it by 1 (decimal: 1% of range). [b]Ctrl[/b] steps 10%. They repeat while held. ✦
[*]A number field applies as you type; a text field on [b]Enter[/b] or when you click away.
[*][b]Rebind[/b] waits for the next key; hold Ctrl, Shift or Alt for a combo, [b]Esc[/b] cancels. No keybind fires meanwhile. Press and release a modifier alone to bind just that modifier. ✦ [b]Block[/b] stops the game also reacting to the combo.
[*]A setting with a fixed set of choices is a dropdown. ✦
[*][b]Saved state[/b] stands for values a plugin keeps by itself, such as where its windows were dragged. [b]Clear[/b] puts them back to their defaults. ✦
[*]The reset arrow restores the default. A plugin's Enabled setting has none, nor does a setting without a default.
[*]The plugin hears each change as you make it. The .ini is written when you let go of a slider or confirm a value. A value a plugin writes to its own .ini shows at once, and Reload picks up settings the new build added.
[/list]

[b][size=4]Settings[/size][/b]

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/{{REPO}}/{{TAG}}/images/settings-tab.png[/img]

[list]
[*]The HUD toggles share one draggable box that remembers where you put it. [b]ModLoader Debug Values[/b] adds input details to it.
[*][b]Keyboard navigation[/b] (on by default) lets Tab, the arrow keys and Space or Enter drive the loader and plugin windows while they are open. Plugin windows over the Escape menu and other game screens with the cursor up do not get it, because the keyboard stays with the game there. ✦
[*][b]Preload images from the game[/b] (off by default) lets the loader read the item and building images that mods list, in the background, before they are drawn. See Image library below. ✦
[*][b]Clear image cache[/b] (a button under Preload images from the game) deletes the images the loader saved to disk, for every game build. Pictures already loaded stay for this session, and the next launch saves them again. ✦
[*][b]Connection Timeout[/b] — see Net timeouts below.
[*][b]Enable Auto Updates[/b] takes effect next launch. A mod loader update asks Yes or No first; a No is remembered for that version.
[*][b]Open Tick Profiler[/b] — [b]Log Tick Stutters[/b] and [b]Stack Sample Stutters[/b] reset on restart. Stack sampling needs stutter logging on and is the heavier of the two.
[/list]

[b][size=4]Theme[/size][/b]

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/{{REPO}}/{{TAG}}/images/theme-tab.png[/img]

[list]
[*][b]Save[/b], [b]Save As...[/b], [b]Delete[/b] and [b]Reload[/b] manage ModLoader\Themes\<name>.ini; share the file to share the theme. [b]Default[/b] and [b]Star Rupture[/b] are built in and read-only: Save As... makes an editable copy. ✦
[*]Colour edits apply at once; Save keeps them and Reload discards them.
[*][b]Font Family[/b] only lists fonts found on your PC. Microsoft YaHei, Meiryo, Malgun Gothic and Arial + CJK add Chinese, Japanese or Korean text.
[/list]

[b][size=4]Logging[/size][/b]

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/{{REPO}}/{{TAG}}/images/logging-tab.png[/img]

[list]
[*][b]Log Level[/b] — the mod loader's own output. [b]Game Log Verbosity[/b] — the game's own log categories, written to StarRupture.log. Both are saved.
[*][b]Per plugin[/b] — one row per plugin and an All plugins row. Turn one plugin up to read it without the rest of the log burying it, or down to silence a noisy one. Default follows the Log Level. These are [b]not saved[/b]: every plugin is back on Default next launch.
[*]A plugin that logs heavily while the game loads has filled the log before you can open the tab. Add a Steam launch option to set its level first: [i]-PluginLogLevel=<plugin>:<level>[/i]. Levels are trace, debug, info, warn, error or default. Separate several with commas, [i]*[/i] sets every plugin you did not name, and quote the whole option if a name has a space.
[/list]

[b][size=4]Pop-ups[/size][/b]

[b]Plugins Updated[/b] appears once at the main menu after an automatic plugin update, and after the mod loader updates itself. After a fresh install the same window is titled [b]Mod Loader Installed[/b].

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/{{REPO}}/{{TAG}}/images/popup-plugins-updated.png[/img]

[b]Plugin Hooks Failed[/b] appears when a plugin did not load because a hook is missing, and again when a later load or reload adds one. [b]Copy Details[/b] puts the report on the clipboard for a bug report. WHY? reopens it.

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/{{REPO}}/{{TAG}}/images/popup-hooks-failed.png[/img]

[b][size=4]Image library[/size][/b] ✦

Mods that show item or building pictures can get them from one place in the loader instead of each copying game textures itself. Mods reach it through IPluginImages (plugin interface v77, ModLoader 1.24.0 or newer).
[list]
[*]A picture is read from the game only when a mod is about to show it.
[*]Preload images from the game, in the Settings tab, is off by default. On, the loader reads the item and building pictures that mods list from the game, one at a time, and pauses while a picture on screen is waiting or video memory is short.
[*]Each picture is stored at 256 px at most. The stored copies are capped at 256 MB, or a small share of your video memory if that is less, and the pictures shown longest ago go first.
[*]Mod icons drawn through the image library are sharper: once a mod keeps drawing an icon at the same size, the loader makes a copy for exactly that size and the mod draws it without resizing. This needs a mod built for it.
[*]Mods that use the image library read game pictures through one shared read that checks the game's texture before it is copied. Mods that still copy textures themselves keep working as before.
[*]Pictures are saved to disk after the first time they are read, and load from there on later launches. A game update, or a mod pak that changed or moved in the load order, starts a new folder. At each launch the loader keeps the folder in use and the newest earlier one, removes the others, and trims the saved pictures to 256 MB, the ones used longest ago first. Pictures of textures the game makes while it runs are not saved. If a mod mounts a pak after the game has started, saved pictures are not used for the rest of that launch.
[*]On a game build whose version object map has no rows for the texture layout, pictures show as placeholders.
[/list]

[b][size=4]Server rules[/size][/b] ✦

A plugin lists its features (a map overlay, a cheat, a new panel) so the loader can switch one off without switching the whole plugin off. A game update does that on its own. A server can do it too, for a feature it does not want on it.
[list]
[*]The server keeps a deny list of plugin and feature pairs. A feature on the list is off, whatever the game build allows. A feature the game build already turned off stays off either way. The list is kept in memory and is empty at every start.
[*]What a player sees: the feature's controls on the plugin's config page are greyed with "<feature> is off: turned off by this server", the plugin shows as [b]Loaded (1 off)[/b], and ModLoader.log says the feature was turned off by this server, and later turned back on. No pop-up opens, and the plugin keeps running without the feature.
[*]The server sends the list to every player whose mod loader sent its feature list, and again when it changes. Leaving the server gives the features back. A player on a server whose mod loader is older gets no list, and ModLoader.log says so once.
[*]A player's mod loader also tells the server which game build it runs. When both builds are known and differ, the server's ModLoader.log gets one warning per join. Nothing is refused, and an older mod loader that sends no build is never reported.
[/list]

[b]features[/b] on the server console or the client's developer console:
[list]
[*][b]features[/b] lists every feature the loaded plugins declared and whether it is [i]on[/i], [i]off: build[/i] or [i]off: server[/i], then the deny list
[*][b]features hostoff <plugin> <id>[/b] adds one feature to the list; the plugin is the name [b]plugins[/b] shows, the id is the one [b]features[/b] shows
[*][b]features hostclear[/b] empties the list
[*][b]features hostmessage <text>[/b] sets the text players get with the list (empty text clears it)
[/list]

For server plugin authors (plugin interface v79, ModLoader 1.27.0 or newer): a server plugin can set the rules from text, for everyone or for one player, one rule per line ([i]default=allow|deny[/i], [i]message=<text>[/i], [i]Plugin.feature=allow|deny[/i], [i]Plugin.*=allow|deny[/i]). It can require a minimum ModLoader version and a minimum version per mod ([i]loader=1.27.0[/i], [i]Plugin=0.11.0[/i], [i]Plugin=wire[/i]); when a player is below, the plugin is called once per join with one line of plain words to show them. The loader never removes anyone: the plugin decides. Players with no mod loader are never checked, and a dev build or a version the loader cannot read is never below. A plugin can also read what a player's loader reported. AskAdmin opens a prompt on a player's screen and the plugin checks the code; the loader does not judge the code and never logs it. These calls do nothing for a server host until a server plugin uses them. The calls do nothing on a client and warn once. A plugin that exports [b]GetPluginWireInfo[/b] says which other versions of itself exchange data with it, so a version bump no longer stops its packets.

[b][size=4]Developer console and server console[/size][/b]

The [b]~[/b] key opens the developer console over the top of the screen; it takes game input while open. Up and Down walk your history. A line the mod loader does not know goes to the game's own console; prefix [b]![/b] to send it there only.

A dedicated server has no UI. Launch it with [b]-console[/b] for a console window with the same commands. Tab completes, Ctrl+C or Ctrl+U clears the line. [b]stop[/b] shuts the server down; [b]close[/b] closes the window and leaves the server running (the X ends the server).

[b]Commands[/b] — [i]<target>[/i] is a plugin name, its index from [b]plugins[/b], or [b]all[/b]. Plugins can add their own; [b]help[/b] lists everything.
[list]
[*][b]help [command][/b], [b]plugins[/b] (every plugin, version and state), [b]version[/b], [b]clear[/b]
[*][b]reload[/b], [b]unload[/b], [b]load[/b] [i]<target>[/i], [b]rescan[/b] (load plugin DLLs added since startup)
[*][b]features[/b] (every plugin feature and its state, and the server's deny list; see Server rules), [b]hookfailures[/b] (plugins whose hook patterns did not resolve), [b]hooklist[/b] (every detoured address and who shares it), [b]preload[/b] (what happened to the DLLs in Preload at startup)
[*][b]loglevel [level][/b], or [b]loglevel <plugin or *> <level or default>[/b] — runtime only
[*][b]pak[/b] — mount and unmount paks, and load, spawn or preview assets from them
[*][b]clients [n][/b] (connected clients and the plugins they reported), [b]nettest [bytes][/b] (round-trip a payload to test fragmentation), [b]stop [force][/b] (server only)
[/list]

[line]

[b][size=6]Installation[/size][/b]

[list=1]
[*]Download the appropriate release (Client or Server).
[*]Extract the files into your game's Binaries Win64 folder.
[/list]
Place the files in the following directory:
[code]StarRupture/Binaries/Win64/[/code]

The ZIP is already laid out correctly, so extract it directly into that folder. Afterwards [i]dwmapi.dll[/i] sits next to the game executable, and everything else the mod loader owns is under [i]ModLoader\[/i]: the core DLL, [i]modloader.ini[/i] and [i]Logs\[/i] (created on first launch), [i]Plugins\[/i] and [i]Preload\[/i]. The client build adds the ImGui DLL and an updater.

[b][size=4]Notes[/size][/b]
[list]
[*]Works for both solo gamers and dedicated servers
[*]Original game files are not modified
[*]Logs and diagnostics are written to [i]ModLoader\Logs\ModLoader.log[/i], keeping the last ten runs
[*]Plugins and the mod loader find the world being played even while another plugin has a preview world of its own open ✦
[*]The loader and plugin windows are drawn into the frames that frame generation makes, so they do not flicker, and frame generation stays on while one is open ✦
[*]Mod windows blink far less when the game gains or loses focus, and ModLoader.log now records focus and window changes and why an overlay frame was skipped ✦
[*]Plugin authors: a window can opt in to being clicked and dragged over the Escape menu and over any game screen that shows the mouse cursor ✦
[*]Plugin windows no longer block the game's own menus while the cursor is up: the Escape menu and other screens with the cursor stay clickable around an open plugin window, and typing in a plugin text field stays out of the game ✦
[*]Plugin authors: plugin interface v73 adds DeclareFeature, so one missing game function or member turns off only that feature, and version map keys look up game functions and members ✦
[*]Plugin authors: plugin interface v74 adds SetNextWindowSize and SetNextWindowSizeConstraints (with named PluginCond_* and PluginWindowFlags_AlwaysAutoResize constants), so a popup no longer collapses into a narrow strip ✦
[*]Plugin authors: plugin interface v74 also gives plugins the game's UI state (in gameplay, pause menu, cursor up, any menu, map, inventory, build menu, station screens) as a change callback (in gameplay, pause menu and cursor up also as a query), so mods no longer detect it themselves; the menu classes follow BetterDrone's menu-layer read and Kian369's MiniMap class list ✦
[*]Plugin authors: plugin interface v74 adds the PLUGIN_FIELD_ANY_SIZE flag for a reflected game member request (size 0), which takes the size the running build reports and returns it, for a member whose type differs between builds (a float on one, a double on the next); a size of 0 without the flag is still refused ✦
[*]Plugin authors: [b]DisablePluginVersionMap=1[/b] under [b][Debug][/b] in modloader.ini gives plugins no version map rows, so you can test a plugin's own patterns and rows; the loader keeps the version map for itself and logs a warning every launch, added at Kian369's request ✦
[*]Plugin authors: [b]RemoteReload=1[/b] under [b][Developer][/b] in modloader.ini lets a script reload a plugin by writing its name to [b]ModLoader\remote_reload.txt[/b], the same reload as the RELOAD button, with the result in ModLoader.log; off by default and logs a warning every launch ✦
[*]Icons in every loader and plugin window now sit on the line of the text beside them ✦
[*]Plugin windows can have their own buttons in the title bar and collapse to it ✦
[*]A plugin window over the Escape menu can leave the menu's buttons clickable under it and still be clickable itself everywhere else ✦
[*]Build Windows Anywhere, Build Benches Anywhere and the MiniMap find their game functions on every game build the loader knows ✦
[*]ModLoader.log no longer warns about every field on a game build that has no version map row of its own and is checked against another build's row ✦
[*]Plugins can ask for the size of a game struct the game does not describe itself; it is read from the version map's row for your game build, and the plugin's feature turns off on a build with no row. The compatibility report lists the struct sizes plugins asked for and is written on any launch where one did ✦
[*]Plugins can ask where the running game puts a game function's parameters and how big its parameter block is, including functions of the game's Blueprint classes (UI widgets, say), so a plugin that calls a game function keeps working when an update adds or moves a parameter. If the Blueprint class is not loaded yet the plugin's feature waits, with a note in ModLoader.log and the compatibility report instead of a warning, and starts once the game loads it ✦
[*]Plugin authors: plugin interface v75 adds [b]titleBarFn[/b] on a panel (your own buttons in its title bar), [b]IsPanelCollapsed[/b] and [b]SetPanelCollapsed[/b] (collapse a panel to its title bar), and the [b]PluginWindowFlags_MenuMouseWhereUncovered[/b] widget flag, which takes the mouse over a game menu only where no menu widget is under the cursor ✦
[/list]

[b][size=4]Game updates[/size][/b]

The mod loader carries a version map: for each game build it lists where the functions and game data it and its plugins read are. A listed build runs with no mod loader update. Plugins that take their game data from the version map run on any listed build; one that does not loads only on the build it was made for.

On a client with Auto Updates on, the mod loader can read a newer version map from a signed file on its GitHub releases (version-map-client.v1.txt, attached to each release, starting with v1.23.2). It checks the signature before reading anything else in the file, and keeps the last good copy in [i]ModLoader\version-map-client.v1.cache[/i]. It looks for a newer one at every start and uses it on that start; with no network the kept copy is used. The version map is read once at startup, after that check. A dedicated server reads its own, separate file the same way (version-map-server.v1.txt, kept as [i]ModLoader\version-map-server.v1.cache[/i]); a client never reads the server's file and a server never reads the client's. The Server release build carries the file's address, [b]Enabled=0[/b] under [b][AutoUpdate][/b] stops that request too, and a server build made without an address fetches nothing and says so in ModLoader.log.

On a client, a game build the version map has no rows for yet does not switch the mod loader off. It runs on the values of the newest build the version map lists and checks each one against your game before using it: a function must be found exactly once and be the kind of place it is expected to be, an address must sit in the right part of the game's code or data, and a class member and its size are taken from the game itself. Something nothing in the game can confirm waits for that build's rows. A mod or feature that needs something that did not check out is switched off, and the window at the main menu says which. Once the version map has rows for your build, the check at the start finds them and they are used instead, on that start; with Auto Updates off that takes a mod loader update.

The mod loader marks the session in [b][UnknownBuild][/b] [i]Session[/i] in modloader.ini and clears it when the game quits normally. If a session ends any other way (a crash, a hang or a forced close), the next start on that build runs without mods, and a dialog says to add [b]Retry=1[/b] under [b][UnknownBuild][/b] to try again. [b]Enabled=0[/b] there keeps the old behaviour: the mod loader stays off on such a build. A dedicated server still stops on a build it does not list, unless its own version map file lists it: the server then runs plugins that take their game data from the version map, and starts without plugins, with no dialog, when the loader cannot find everything it needs (ModLoader.log says what). The server hands plugins the same Game-SDK globals as the client when the version map serves the running server build.

At startup the loader also checks that the game classes it reads are laid out as it was built for. It carries a layout table for each game build it supports (CL-127004 and CL-130045) and checks the running game against the table of its own build, or against the newest one when the build has no table of its own. If a game update changes a layout, only the features that read it are switched off, not every plugin. If a class every plugin depends on changed, or the game's ProcessEvent moved, all plugins stay off. The dedicated server runs the same check against a table made from the server's own game files, and starts with no plugins if a class its loader reads has moved. The compatibility report is written by the client only.

Even when the version map decides where a plugin's function is, the mod loader checks the plugin's own pattern against the running game once and writes a warning to ModLoader.log if it matches nowhere or in two or more places. The plugin still loads; the warning lets its author find a pattern that only works because the version map covers for it. ✦ The loader's pattern scan cache is now kept per game exe (version, build timestamp and size), so a rebuilt exe with the same version string rescans instead of trusting an old result. ✦

[b][size=4]Installing Plugins[/size][/b]
The mod loader ships with no plugins. Download the ones you want and drop their .dll into [i]ModLoader\Plugins\[/i]. Client plugins only load on a client, server plugins only on a dedicated server; the window says so if you get one the wrong way round. If a plugin has an Enabled setting, it can be turned on in the Config tab, or in its .ini under [i]Plugins\config\[/i]. This mod loader accepts plugin interface 70 to 79.

If a plugin ships a .json file alongside its .dll, it can update itself: the mod loader checks for a new version at startup and downloads it. The Plugins tab marks those with a green dot (amber means the plugin has no update manifest and has to be updated by hand). Auto-updating can be turned off in the Settings tab.

[b][size=4]Linux Users[/size][/b]
If the modloader isnt working, you may have to set the following envrionment variable via the startup parameters in Steam:
[code]WINEDLLOVERRIDES="dwmapi=n,b" %command%[/code]

[line]

[b][size=6]Configuration[/size][/b]

Config file: [i]ModLoader\modloader.ini[/i], created on first launch. The Settings, Logging and Theme tabs write most of it for you.

[list]
[*][b]Logging[/b] — Level (INFO by default; TRACE, DEBUG, INFO, WARN or ERROR), EnableFile, FileName, GameVerbosity, GameLogCategories (servers only; empty leaves the game log alone)
[*][b]UI[/b] — Enabled (0 turns the overlay off), OffAfterDeviceLoss (the loader sets it after a graphics device loss: the overlay stays off for the next launch only, then the key is removed), OpenKey (F2), GameMenuEntry (0 removes the MOD LOADER menu row), FontScale (0.75 to 5.00), FontFamily, Theme
[*][b]Images[/b] — Preload (0; 1 reads the picture sets that mods list in the background)
[*][b]Console[/b] — OpenKey (Tilde)
[*][b]Input[/b] — SuppressKeybindsWhileTyping (1): keybinds ignore keys typed into a game text box
[*][b]Network[/b] — ConnectionTimeout, InitialConnectTimeout (300 seconds each; 1 to 3600, 0 keeps the engine default)
[*][b]AutoUpdate[/b] — Enabled (1; 0 also stops the check for a newer version object map at every start, on a dedicated server too), ManifestUrl (empty uses the built-in manifest)
[*][b]UnknownBuild[/b] — Enabled (1; 0 keeps the mod loader off on a game build the version object map does not list), Retry (1 tries mods again after a session on such a build did not end cleanly; the loader removes it at that start), Session (written by the loader while a session runs; don't edit it)
[*][b]Preload[/b] — Enabled (1), Disabled (comma-separated DLL names to skip)
[*][b]UE4SS[/b] — Enabled (0), Path
[/list]

[b][size=4]Command-line switches[/size][/b]
[list]
[*][b]-console[/b] — open the console window (also [b]-mlconsole[/b], [b]-modloaderconsole[/b]). Works on a client too, without stop
[*][b]-PluginLogLevel=<plugin>:<level>[/b] — set plugin log levels before they load
[*][b]-NoPreload[/b] — skip the preload phase
[*][b]-SkipPatternValidation[/b] — start even if a scan pattern is ambiguous or the wrong kind
[/list]

[b][size=4]Net timeouts[/size][/b]
A player can be dropped while the host loads a map: the host's game thread does not tick during the load, so the client sees silence, and the engine's stock 60 second timeout disconnects everyone still joining. The mod loader raises both net timeouts to [b]300 seconds[/b]; the Settings tab has a slider and shows what the live net driver is using. [b]Both ends need it[/b], and a genuinely dead connection now holds its player slot for the full timeout.

[b][size=4]Preload plugins[/size][/b]
A preload plugin is a DLL in [i]ModLoader\Preload\[/i] that runs before the game executable's entry point, to patch code the engine touches on its way up. One that fails is unloaded, reported, and the game boots without it. If you only need engine events, UI, config or networking, use an ordinary plugin instead: it can be hot-reloaded while the game runs. Plugin authors: see the [url=https://github.com/AlienXAXS/StarRupture-Plugin-SDK]Plugin SDK[/url] and the [url=https://github.com/AlienXAXS/StarRupture-PreLoadPlugin-SDK]PreLoad Plugin SDK[/url].

[line]

[b][size=6]Troubleshooting[/size][/b]

[list]
[*][b]Nothing loads at all[/b] — dwmapi.dll must sit next to the game .exe with the ModLoader folder beside it, not in a subfolder of its own. No Logs\Proxy.log at all means the game never loaded dwmapi.dll
[*][b]Plugins not loading[/b] — the DLLs must be in ModLoader\Plugins\ with Enabled set in the plugin's .ini where it has one. The Plugins tab shows why a plugin was rejected
[*][b]Game will not start after adding a preload plugin[/b] — launch with -NoPreload, or set Enabled=0 in the Preload section of modloader.ini. The loader also skips a preload plugin that did not finish loading last launch, and says which. Delete Preload\.state to let it try again
[*][b]A plugin says "Needs an update to run on CL-..."[/b] — the plugin was built for another game build and does not take its game data from the mod loader's version map, so it would read this game's data where its own build had it. Ask its author for an update
[*][b]ModLoader.log says a plugin's "own pattern matches N times"[/b] — the plugin loads, because the version map decides where its function is. But the pattern it carries as a backup is not unique on this game build (or, for 0 times, no longer found), and a mod loader with no row to use for it (a stock mod loader, a dedicated server for a key its table lacks, or a build the version map's rows do not resolve on) would refuse the plugin. Tell its author to make the pattern unique or update it ✦
[*][b]A dialog says the game has changed in a way that would break your plugins[/b] — a game update moved some game classes the mod loader and its plugins read, so no plugins load this session. Start the game without them or wait for an update. The loader writes [i]ModLoader\compat-report-<CL>.json[/i] (the game build and what moved); attach it to your report
[*][b]A dialog says the game did not finish starting within 120 seconds[/b] — the mod loader waits up to 2 minutes for the game to finish starting, then checks the game's layout. On a game build the mod loader was not made for, a game that has not finished starting by then cannot be checked, so no plugins load this session. The game may be stuck on a message box of its own, or its files may be damaged: verify the game files in Steam, then start again. ModLoader.log has the line [i]The game did not finish starting within 120 seconds[/i]
[*][b]Plugin data does not show up on one side, or the plugin waits for server data[/b] — a plugin's data only crosses between a server and a player when their copies of it can read each other's packets. Search ModLoader\Logs\ModLoader.log, on the server and on the player's PC, for [i][NetworkChannel] Plugin '<name>'[/i]. [i]Wire format not known to match, plugin data will not be exchanged[/i] means the two run different versions of that plugin and the plugin has not said they work together: put the same version on both sides, or update both to a build whose author lists the other version as compatible. [i]Plugin data is exchanged because they are compatible[/i] is only a note that two different versions were accepted. [i]The server's mod loader sends no plugin wire table[/i] is logged once on the player's PC and means the server's mod loader is older than this feature: it still needs identical plugin versions, so match the versions exactly or update the server's mod loader
[*][b]A dialog offers "Start Game Without ModLoader"[/b] — the game build changed or a scan pattern no longer matches. The mod loader stays off until it is updated. Copy Details has the report. For a game build the version object map does not list, this dialog appears only when [b]Enabled=0[/b] is set under [b][UnknownBuild][/b], when the game's version has no CL number, or when the mod loader cannot write modloader.ini
[*][b]UNKNOWN GAME BUILD at the main menu[/b] — the game updated to a build the version object map does not list yet. The mod loader is running on checked values from the newest build it lists, and the window says what, if anything, was switched off. Nothing to do: once the version map has rows for your build, the check at every start finds them and uses them
[*][b]A dialog says the last session on an unknown game build did not end cleanly[/b] — the game crashed, stopped responding or was closed without quitting the last time it ran on this build with the mod loader on checked values, so mods are off for this start. To try again, add [b]Retry=1[/b] under [b][UnknownBuild][/b] in ModLoader\modloader.ini and start the game
[*][b]Logs and diagnostics[/b] — check ModLoader\Logs\ModLoader.log; raise the detail in the Logging tab
[*][b]The game's log is flooding on a dedicated server[/b] — the game itself writes hundreds of LogTemp and LogSkinnedMeshComp warnings a second. Server builds turn those two categories down to errors; change them with GameLogCategories in modloader.ini
[*][b]Game stops responding to input[/b] — turn on ModLoader Debug Values in the Settings tab; it names the plugin holding input open
[*][b]Game freezes ("Not Responding")[/b] — look near the end of ModLoader\Logs\ModLoader.log for a [PluginWatchdog] line. A plugin's tick or overlay draw callback still running after 5 seconds is named there. Disable that plugin and report it to its author
[*][b]The game crashes[/b] — the mod loader's own dialog has Copy Details and buttons for ModLoader.log and StarRupture.log. Include them in a bug report
[*][b]Players dropped during a long map load[/b] — see Net timeouts. Both ends need the mod loader
[/list]

